{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793568620,"nonce":"A3YRQIsgvuxEv7pwqPayPAEqPW8=","title":"Concatenated strings and byte strings are built one piece at a time","message":"A chain of `+`, an f-string, `%` or `str.format` joined its pieces two at a time, so each step allocated an intermediate result, and a join with a byte string that was not ASCII checked the whole result for UTF-8 into a second copy. chaos spends most of its wall time after the timed loop, writing one `b'%c%c%c' % (c, c, c)` per pixel: 1.2M pixels at about 120 ns each on the NUC (CPython 125 ns). A disk file's buffer also grew by pushing one zero byte at a time.\n\nFixed: `zrtl::string::string_concat_n` joins any number of pieces in one allocation (TEXT when every piece is TEXT or ASCII, a byte string when a piece holds a byte no UTF-8 text can, the joined bytes decide otherwise); the Python frontend sends runs of 3 to 8 pieces to `zb_str_concat3..8`, flattening `a + b + c` chains; the file buffer grows with `resize_filled`.\n\nNUC, 4 pinned cores, interleaved: the pixel loop 120 to 65 ms steady (cold first run 143 to 81 ms); chaos wall 266 to 205 ms; telco -4%; the other string-heavy kernels within noise. Heap (anon RSS) unchanged.","files":null}]}